#: app/src/gui/view/evidence.ts:163
msgid ""
"The domain that signed this message, taken from the signature itself. It is "
"the domain whose published key has to check out, and it is not necessarily "
"the domain in the From line."
msgstr ""

#: app/src/gui/view/evidence.ts:167
msgid ""
"The domain in the From line, which is what a reader sees as the sender. A "
"signature from a different domain proves only that the other domain handled "
"the message."
msgstr ""

#: app/src/gui/view/evidence.ts:171
msgid ""
"Whether the signing domain and the From domain are the same. Aligned means "
"the sender signed for itself. Not aligned means somebody else did, such as a "
"mailing list or a mail provider."
msgstr ""

#: app/src/gui/view/evidence.ts:175
msgid ""
"Which of a domain’s keys signed this message. A domain publishes several and "
"names each one, so the selector plus the domain is the address of the public "
"key."
msgstr ""

#: app/src/gui/view/evidence.ts:179
msgid ""
"The hash and the signature algorithm used, such as rsa-sha256. SHA-1 is "
"broken, so a signature made with rsa-sha1 carries a caveat even when it "
"verifies."
msgstr ""

#: app/src/gui/view/evidence.ts:183
msgid ""
"How much harmless reformatting the signature tolerates. Relaxed forgives "
"rewrapped whitespace, simple forgives nothing, and mail servers reformat "
"messages in transit all the time."
msgstr ""

#: app/src/gui/view/evidence.ts:187
msgid ""
"The public key that checked the signature: its size, and where this case got "
"it. A key that came from the archive under examination proves the archive is "
"consistent with itself, not that the message is genuine."
msgstr ""

#: app/src/gui/view/evidence.ts:191
msgid ""
"The header fields the signature covers. Any field not in this list could "
"have been changed or added afterwards without breaking the signature, and "
"Subject, To and Date are the ones that matter."
msgstr ""

#: app/src/gui/view/evidence.ts:195
msgid ""
"The fingerprint of the body the sender signed, against the fingerprint this "
"message’s body produces. Both are shown when they differ, so somebody else "
"can repeat the comparison by hand."
msgstr ""

#: app/src/gui/view/evidence.ts:199
msgid ""
"Whether the signature itself checks out against the public key. This covers "
"the header fields listed above; the body is covered by the body hash."
msgstr ""

#: app/src/gui/view/evidence.ts:203
msgid ""
"An l= tag, which signs only the first so many bytes of the body. Everything "
"after that is unsigned and can be appended by anyone without breaking the "
"signature."
msgstr ""

#: app/src/gui/view/evidence.ts:207
msgid ""
"Why no verdict was possible. Almost always a missing public key, which says "
"nothing against the message: fetch the key and it gets a verdict."
msgstr ""

#: app/src/gui/view/evidence.ts:211
msgid ""
"The wording of the library that did the cryptography, kept as it came. It is "
"the line that separates two findings Stamped reports under the same reason, "
"and a bug report without it cannot be acted on."
msgstr ""

#: app/src/gui/view/evidence.ts:231
msgid ""
"DKIM lets a domain sign the messages it sends. The sending server hashes the "
"body and some of the headers, signs the result with a private key, and "
"publishes the matching public key in the DNS. Anyone holding that public key "
"can check that the signed parts are unchanged. A signature that verifies "
"proves the message left that domain in this form. It does not prove who "
"wrote it, and it says nothing about the parts the signature does not cover."
msgstr ""

